-- `p's adult` and `adult p` read one field of one record: the projection is sugar
-- for the selector applied, and the evaluator reads both as one field read
-- (smucclaw/l4-ide#1033, gap 3). Keyed as two atoms, `l4 verify` could not see
-- that `either spelling contradicts` asks for the field and its negation at once.
-- `two people` is the control: the same shape over two records is satisfiable.
--
-- A computed field is read through to its MEANS whichever way it is written, so
-- its contradiction is found when the projection comes first, and when it is
-- the only spelling. And a rule that reads the ledger, called by projection
-- either side of a RECORD, is two reads, not one: `ledger by projection` is
-- satisfiable (its #EVAL is TRUE). All three found by adversarial review.

DECLARE Person HAS adult IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `either spelling contradicts` IF p's adult AND NOT adult p

GIVEN p IS A Person
      q IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `two people` IF p's adult AND NOT adult q

DECLARE Person2 HAS age IS A NUMBER
                    grown IS A BOOLEAN MEANS age >= 18

GIVEN p IS A Person2
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `computed, projection first` IF p's grown AND grown p AND NOT (p's age >= 18)

GIVEN p IS A Person2
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `computed, projection only` IF p's grown AND NOT (p's age >= 18)

DECLARE Acct HAS id IS A NUMBER

GIVEN a IS AN Acct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is recorded` a IF (RECALL `x`) EQUALS JUST 7

GIVEN a IS AN Acct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ledger by projection` IF
        NOT a's `is recorded`
    AND (RECORD `x` IS 7) EQUALS 7
    AND a's `is recorded`

#EVAL `ledger by projection` (Acct WITH id IS 1)
